374eefb00a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488753 #edb/ref 1080 "All the statements are true" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488753 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488753 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8d4caa60-0479-5815-b2a0-47647bc7550b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488821 #edb/ref 1080 "IV only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488821 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488821 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b00e08ba-08b5-5691-9161-9a7d57702f37" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488895 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488895 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488895 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d0dc412d-2d7f-5a7d-9147-f6af3bd08779" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488915 #edb/ref 1080 "III and IV only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488915 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488915 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d7a005c0-68dc-5657-b99c-da3dd9395ea0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489112 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186488634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489112 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186488652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489112 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186488744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489112 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186488821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489112 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186488895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489112 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489112 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489112 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186488821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489112 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "af22d48e-5724-584d-b3a5-2b9d70fcf5d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489154 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186488532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489154 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186488594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489154 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186488703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489154 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186488753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489154 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186488915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489154 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489154 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489154 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186488753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489154 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ecd11032-a320-56b0-8408-67c72e266d26" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489173 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186489154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489173 #edb/ref 1069 "q-157467" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489173 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489173 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $z=3x+2y$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $Π$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489173 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "00c50921-42ae-53d0-a1bc-1a992257ffe3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489173 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ 3x+2y\\end{aligned}],\\,x,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $z=3x+2y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =3x+2y \\\\ (0) & =3(0)+2(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 3{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ 3{b}_{1}+2{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 3{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ 3{b}_{1}+2{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ (3{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2})+(3{b}_{1}+2{b}_{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ 3({a}_{1}+{b}_{1})+2({a}_{2}+{b}_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=3x+2y.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 3{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 3{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k(3{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ 3k{a}_{1}+2k{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=3x+2y.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are true, the plane $Π$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489255 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186489112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489255 #edb/ref 1069 "q-156680" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489255 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489255 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(1,-1,2)$ and $(-1,1,1).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489255 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a5bfe454-c84f-5da8-9381-6959e5f62f7a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489255 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\frac{x-1}{-2}=\\frac{y+1}{2}=\\frac{z-2}{-1}.\n$$\n\nBut this line does not pass through the origin since \n\n$$\n\\frac{(0)-1}{-2}=\\frac{(0)+1}{2}\\ne \\frac{(0)-2}{-1}\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider the vectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ from $L$ (the position vectors of the given points). Then,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHowever, the point $(0,0,3)$ does not belong to $L$ since\n\n$$\n\\frac{(0)-1}{-2}=\\frac{(0)+1}{2}\\ne -(3)+2.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489304 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "21466245-f430-5f32-bab1-95d0310fd556" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489304 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186489173 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489318 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186489346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489318 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "50364cf2-ba89-551c-8162-eed479c1cceb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489318 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186120153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489320 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186489304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489320 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "562229c5-8d32-5b9a-8f54-0759c5f5092c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489320 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186275396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489339 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186489318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489339 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8ef378a7-58e1-50e8-86be-81d99657fd29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489339 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186489255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489346 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186489320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489346 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9e51c793-12d9-5aac-a409-0a574ae80bab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489346 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186275390 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
